  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA moderate pressure gradient has developed across the central\nplains, as a high pressure center has continued to strengthen\nthrough the Midwest. This pressure gradient is expected to \nincrease into the afternoon, as an approaching shortwave trough\ncauses the pressure to fall across the Rocky Mountains and \nsouthern plains. This pressure gradient will allow gusty south- \nsoutheasterly winds through the afternoon, with gusts of 25-30 \nMPH possible. Additionally, a band of low-level clouds \ncurrently over western Nebraska and western Kansas looks to be \nexpanding slightly to the east. High temperatures will stay \nsuppressed in the low 40s under this cloud cover (particularly \nalong and west of Highway 183). Outside of these clouds, \nsunshine and strong southerly winds will allow temperatures to \nclimb into the upper 40s to low 50s this afternoon. \n\nAs the shortwave trough continues to move eastward tonight, a\nbroad region of precipitation will develop in the southern\nplains. Current model guidance has continued to show the\nnorthern fringe of this system reaching up into central Kansas,\nwith a small chance of this precipitation reaching up into our \nnorthern Kansas counties. Aside from these precipitation \nchances, this system will bring some low-level clouds across \nmuch of the area tonight, allowing lows to fall only into the \nlow to mid 30s. \n\nAfter this wave moves across the plains, a broad upper level\nridge will build across the central CONUS. This ridge will \nallow for a dry, warming period to start the week, as high \ntemperatures increase to near 50 tomorrow and peak into the 60s \nWednesday afternoon. Along with this warming trend, the surface \nhigh pressure center will expand across the Mississippi Valley, \nallowing some breezy southerly winds to continue through the \nbeginning of the week. South-southeasterly winds will gust up to\n30-35 MPH tomorrow and Monday. As the next system starts to\ncause some pressure falls in the higher terrain Tuesday, these \nwinds will increase, with sustained southerly winds around 20-25\nMPH and gusts up to 40-45 MPH possible Tuesday and Wednesday \nafternoon.\n\nA more active weather pattern returns towards the end of next\nweek. First, there is a slight (10-15%) chance of rain \nWednesday night into Thursday, as a disturbance crosses the \narea. However, more promising precipitation chances return next \nweekend (roughly 60-80% chance), as a more prominent trough \nmoves into the area. Most model guidance is in agreement that \nsome sort of low pressure system will develop over the central \nplains sometime Friday or Saturday. However, there is some \ndisagreement on the timing and location of this system.",
